[{:seon.task/name "a-merged-tree-is-analyzed-in-a-fork"
  :seon.task/plan "Not ready: first measure what refresh-tx in root catches that a member's own import and seon.merge/dangling do not; if nothing, delete unanalyzable instead; Optimization measures, numbers to the owner Sean before any merge-blocking check. From an independent audit of Context's merges (2026-11-08), approved by the owner. Done: a merge that leaves a name unbound (an octopus member calling what another removes, or one branch removing what main still calls) is refused from the recorded references, with no process (docs/merging.md#a-merge-leaves-no-name-unbound). Left: seon.writer/unanalyzable runs source/refresh-tx in root's own image, so before a merge is decided root evaluates the candidate's ns form (which can require), its defmacros, unaliases live namespaces and flips a live var's :private (seon.source/alias-held-not-declared guards one symptom). Learned 2026-11-08: a process loading the merged program from main's (seon.program/load-diff!, as an agent resumed on a branch does) does not see a removed name called as x/f, since Jolt compiles a name under an alias with no var as a lookup at each call; it does see an unqualified one and a changed macro's callers. A child that loads needs no new mechanism: an agent with no task spawned on a branch holding the candidate runs load-diff! at start and its hello carries :load-errors (root's set-agent would answer them with the spawn). Before building, measure what refresh-tx in root catches that a member's own import (analyzed in its own process when recorded) and seon.merge/dangling do not; if nothing seen, delete unanalyzable and alias-held-not-declared rather than move them. A merge-blocking check: its numbers go to the owner before it lands."
